  "evidence": "The quote itself is verbatim-accurate (verified by fetching w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/grid/), but the claim misreads its role in the source. In the actual APG text, that sentence is not presented as a standalone 'documented harm of the grid choice' — it is the RATIONALE for a mandatory design requirement stated in the same paragraph: 'In a grid, every cell contains a focusable element or is itself focusable, regardless of whether the cell content is editable or interactive... One reason it is important for all cells to be able to receive or contain keyboard focus is that screen readers will typically be in their application reading mode...' The APG's own fix for the exact problem the claim describes is baked into the pattern's authoring requirement itself (make all cells focusable), so a correctly-implemented grid per the pattern does not have unfocusable content silently skipped — the harm is contingent on NOT following the pattern's own mandate, not an inherent property of 'the grid choice.' The claim omits this mitigating requirement and reframes rationale-for-a-rule as an unavoidable design trade-off. Second, the claim's closing framing — 'the wrong answer for content-heavy tabular data' — does not match the primary source's actual grid-vs-table criterion, which WebFetch confirmed is interactivity/editability ('particularly useful if the tabular information is editable or interactive'), not content density; 'content-heavy' is an unsupported extrapolation. Third, I searched for independent corroboration (WebSearch for TPGi/Deque coverage of this specific mechanism) and found none beyond restatements of the same single W3C sentence; the one independent critique found (Adrian Roselli, 'ARIA Grid As an Anti-Pattern') argues grid is misapplied to static data but for different reasons (tab-stop confusion, inconsistent AT support, virtual-cursor bypass) — it does not corroborate the specific 'silently skipped non-focusable content' causal story as a standalone documented harm of choosing grid.",
  "confidence": "medium"
}
